⚙️ My Custom Engineering New Tab

I got super tired of the messy default Chrome new tab page distracting me, so I built this clean dashboard for my engineering classes and coding projects. It puts all the sites and tools I actually use right in front of me so I can just get to work!

🌐 Try it Live!
        |
        v
 https://pixelprise543.github.io/Enginnering-New-Tab/ 
 
 I actually deployed this whole project on GitHub Pages too! That means you can check out the dashboard and see how it works as a normal website without even installing the extension first.

👉 Click here to see the live version!

✨ What It Does

🔍 Quick Search: Easily switch between Google, ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Wolfram Alpha right from the main search bar using the actual brand logos.

🛠️ Engineering Shortcuts: One-click buttons to the stuff I use most for my projects: Onshape, GrabCAD, McMaster-Carr, GitHub, and SolidProfessor.

⏱️ Study Timer: A built-in Pomodoro timer to help me focus on homework. You can set it to 5, 15, 25, or 50 minutes.

✅ To-Do List: A simple checklist for assignments and project due dates. It saves automatically!

📝 Quick Notes: A little scratchpad to type down formulas or random ideas. It remembers what you wrote even if you close the tab.

⛅ Weather & Clock: Shows the local weather, plus the date and time so I don't lose track of time.

🎨 Blueprint Theme: Has a really cool blueprint grid background that looks like actual engineering paper.

💻 How I Built It

I made this entirely from scratch! I wanted it to be super fast, so I didn't use any huge templates or heavy frameworks.

Frontend: Just plain HTML5 and CSS3.

Styling: Custom CSS variables, CSS Grids, and Flexbox.

Coding: Vanilla JavaScript.

Saving Data: Uses your browser's localStorage so you don't even need an account or database to save your notes.

APIs: The Open-Meteo API grabs the live weather based on where you are.

Hosting: Deployed live on the web using GitHub Pages!

🚀 How to Install It

Since this is my custom extension, it's not on the Chrome store yet. You just load it locally on your computer:

Download the code: Clone this repository or download the .zip file and unzip it.

Open Extensions: Open Google Chrome and type chrome://extensions/ in the URL bar.

Enable Developer Mode: Turn on Developer mode in the top right corner of the screen.

Load the Extension: Click the Load unpacked button in the top left corner.

Select the Folder: Choose the folder you just downloaded (make sure it's the folder that has the manifest.json file inside).

Open a New Tab: Open a brand new tab and check it out!

⌨️ Keyboard Shortcuts

I added these so you don't even have to use your mouse:

Shortcut

What it does

/

Jumps straight to the Search Bar

T

Switches between Light and Dark mode

N

Jumps to the Scratchpad so you can type a note

Space

Starts or pauses the study timer

🧠 Why I Made This

Since I'm a student taking engineering and maker classes, I found myself clicking the same 5 websites every single day. The normal new tab page was basically useless to me. I wanted to make something that actually helped me get my homework and CAD projects done faster, while also teaching myself how to build real Chrome extensions, save local data, code CSS grids, and deploy to GitHub!